From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: h713 <h713@local>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 03:11:14 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] aic8800: unregister the SDIO driver when aic8800_bsp unloads

rmmod of aic8800_bsp, then insmod of a new aic8800_bsp and aic8800_fdrv,
oopsed the LPDDR3 projector in the next SDIO enumeration and hard-hung it:

    Workqueue: events_freezable mmc_rescan
    pc : __device_attach_driver+0x20/0x1bc
     __device_attach_driver
     __device_attach
     bus_probe_device
     device_add
     sdio_add_func
     mmc_attach_sdio
     mmc_rescan
    Modules linked in: aic8800_fdrv(O+) aic8800_bsp(O) ...
                       [last unloaded: aic8800_bsp(O)]

aicbsp_exit() only calls aicbsp_sdio_exit() under CONFIG_PLATFORM_ROCKCHIP.
On every other platform the driver is unregistered only by
aicbsp_set_subsys() when *all* subsystems have powered off, and not at all on
init's probe-timeout path (aicbsp_sdio_init() returns with the driver
registered and err1 goes straight to power-off). So the unloaded module's
sdio_driver stayed on the bus in freed memory, and the bus walk of the next
enumeration dereferenced it.

Unregister on exit for CONFIG_PLATFORM_MAINLINE_SUNXI too, before
aicbsp_mainline_cleanup() drops wlan_regon so remove() still sees a powered
chip. remove() is the vendor's own teardown, the one the all-off path already
runs. driver_unregister() does not clear drv->p, so a second unregister would
be a use-after-free of its own: aicbsp_sdio_exit() now tracks whether the
driver is registered and unregisters at most once, which also makes the
existing all-off path and the new exit path safe in either order.

aicbsp_mainline_cleanup() then cuts wlan_regon, but mmc1 is not
non-removable, so the MMC core kept the dead card's functions until the next
detect. The next load's dummy driver bound them, and sdio_bus_probe() timed
out setting the block size before the vendor probe ran:

    aicbsp_dummy_sdmmc mmc1:6721:1: probe with driver aicbsp_dummy_sdmmc
    failed with error -110

(the power-on rescan then removed the card and enumerated it fresh). Cleanup
now calls aicbsp_mainline_rescan() after cutting power, as
aicbsp_platform_power_off() already does, so the card is gone at unload.
---
 aic8800_bsp/aic_bsp_main.c | 10 ++++++++++
 aic8800_bsp/aicsdio.c      | 20 ++++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 30 insertions(+)

diff --git a/aic8800_bsp/aic_bsp_main.c b/aic8800_bsp/aic_bsp_main.c
index f959e62..f3a5558 100644
--- a/aic8800_bsp/aic_bsp_main.c
+++ b/aic8800_bsp/aic_bsp_main.c
@@ -456,6 +456,16 @@ static void __exit aicbsp_exit(void)
     if(aicbsp_sdiodev){
     	aicbsp_sdio_exit();
     }
+#endif
+#ifdef CONFIG_PLATFORM_MAINLINE_SUNXI
+	/*
+	 * aicbsp_set_subsys() only unregisters when every subsystem has powered
+	 * off, and init's probe-timeout path never does. Either way the driver
+	 * would outlive this module on the SDIO bus and the next enumeration
+	 * would walk into freed memory. Before cleanup cuts wlan_regon, so
+	 * remove() still talks to a powered chip.
+	 */
+	aicbsp_sdio_exit();
 #endif
 	sysfs_remove_group(&(aicbsp_pdev->dev.kobj), &aicbsp_attribute_group);
 	platform_device_del(aicbsp_pdev);
diff --git a/aic8800_bsp/aicsdio.c b/aic8800_bsp/aicsdio.c
index e0838a7..f3f2bad 100644
--- a/aic8800_bsp/aicsdio.c
+++ b/aic8800_bsp/aicsdio.c
@@ -157,6 +157,15 @@ void aicbsp_mainline_cleanup(void)
 		aicbsp_wlan_regon_requested = false;
 		pr_info("aicbsp: wlan_regon GPIO %d released\n",
 			aicbsp_wlan_regon_gpio);
+		/*
+		 * The chip is off now, but mmc1 is not non-removable, so the core
+		 * keeps the card until something asks it to look. Ask now, as
+		 * aicbsp_platform_power_off() does; otherwise the next load's
+		 * dummy driver binds the dead functions and sdio_bus_probe()
+		 * times out setting the block size (-110). The rescan runs in
+		 * the core's own work, so nothing of this module is left in it.
+		 */
+		aicbsp_mainline_rescan();
 	}
 	if (aicbsp_wlan_hostwake_gpio >= 0)
 		gpio_free(aicbsp_wlan_hostwake_gpio);
@@ -819,12 +828,20 @@ static void aicbsp_platform_power_off(void)
 }
 
 
+/*
+ * Whether aicbsp_sdio_driver is on the SDIO bus. driver_unregister() leaves
+ * drv->p dangling, so a second unregister is a use-after-free: every caller
+ * goes through aicbsp_sdio_exit(), which unregisters at most once.
+ */
+static bool aicbsp_sdio_registered;
+
 int aicbsp_sdio_init(void)
 {
 
 	if (sdio_register_driver(&aicbsp_sdio_driver)) {
 		return -1;
 	} else {
+		aicbsp_sdio_registered = true;
 		//may add mmc_rescan here
 	}
 	if (down_timeout(&aicbsp_probe_semaphore, msecs_to_jiffies(2000)) != 0){
@@ -838,7 +855,10 @@ int aicbsp_sdio_init(void)
 
 void aicbsp_sdio_exit(void)
 {
+	if (!aicbsp_sdio_registered)
+		return;
 	sdio_unregister_driver(&aicbsp_sdio_driver);
+	aicbsp_sdio_registered = false;
 }
 
 void aicbsp_sdio_release(struct aic_sdio_dev *sdiodev)
